// =====================================================================
// Solution C - date dimension with fiscal year columns (full version)
//
// Self-contained. This folder shares no AML object with Solutions A, B
// or D - only the untouchable core-demo base models, which every
// solution extends and none of them edits. Solution D has its own,
// plainer copy of this file; the two are deliberately independent so
// either can be demoed on its own.
//
// Extends the shared `dim_dates` rather than copying it, so the base
// calendar (one row per day, 2013 to today) stays owned by
// `01 demo ecommerce/` and is not modified here.
//
// Seed Company's fiscal year runs 1 Oct to 30 Sep, named by the ENDING
// calendar year: 2026 = 1 Oct 2025 -> 30 Sep 2026.
//
// This is the ONLY file in this solution that knows what a fiscal
// year is. Fact tables carry no fiscal fields, metrics carry no fiscal
// logic. Adding a new fact entity costs one relationship line in the
// dataset; adding a new metric costs nothing at all.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Everything here is @sql, and that is deliberate
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// An earlier version of this file wrote these columns in AQL as
// `case(when: cast(seed_c_dates.month_number, 'number') >= 10, ...)`.
// That works, but it does not survive `extend()`: the inherited copy
// still points at `seed_c_dates`, so every role model built on top of
// this one has to re-declare all of them. Measured: extending this
// model with an empty body produced 9 x E102 "Model, metric, or field
// `seed_c_dates` is not defined".
//
// Written as @sql against `{{ #SOURCE.col }}` and bare sibling
// references like `{{ fiscal_year }}`, there is no model name to go
// stale, and a role model costs zero re-declared fields. That is what
// makes the second-date-field case cheap — see `seed_c_dates_signup`.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Built on `month_number` / `year`, NOT on date arithmetic
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// The obvious derivation counts months from an anchor Oct 1 with
// date_diff. It is off by a whole month. `date_key` is a DATE, and
// Holistics casts it to timestamptz then applies the tenant timezone,
// which rolls every month-start back into the previous month, while the
// anchor literal carries its own offset and does not shift. Verified:
// it produced fiscal years running 1 Nov to 31 Oct.
//
// `month_number` and `year` are `to_char()` output from the base query,
// evaluated in the warehouse on the raw date, so they carry no timezone
// distortion. The same reasoning applies to the join key on the fact
// side — see the note in `seed_c.dataset.aml`.
//
// SQL portability: CAST(... AS INT), CASE, `||`, LPAD and EXTRACT are valid on
// Postgres, Snowflake and Redshift, which covers Seed either way
// (Snowflake per Lori, Redshift per Chukwudi). On BigQuery, INT becomes
// INT64.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Current fiscal year, with no date written down
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// `fiscal_year_offset` compares each row's fiscal year against the
// fiscal year containing CURRENT_DATE. It is a row-level expression, not
// an aggregate, which is the whole point: an aggregate like
// `fiscal_year - max(fiscal_year)` re-bases whenever a filter narrows
// the query, so a card filtered to FY2024 would report FY2024 as
// "current". Verified by reading the generated SQL — a filter on the
// FACT side also reaches the dimension-side aggregate, via a semi-join
// CTE the MAX() is then INNER JOINed against. The row-level form has no
// such scope to be narrowed.
//
// That fixes the offset reporting the wrong year. It does not by itself
// make a pinned card survive a dashboard filter — a filter still
// intersects with the metric's own where(). `of_all()` handles that half,
// in the metric rather than here; see `seed_c.dataset.aml`.
//
// CURRENT_DATE is a keyword, not an inlined value, so the SQL text is
// stable and the query cache still hits — unlike @now, which inlines a
// full timestamp and changes the SQL on every execution. That matters
// for a cost-sensitive customer. The one caveat is that a result cached
// before 1 October is stale after it; the base `dim_dates` query already
// has this property, since its generate_series ends at current_date.
// =====================================================================
Model seed_c_dates = dim_dates.extend({
  label: 'Seed Dates (Solution C)'
  description: 'Shared date dimension plus Seed Company fiscal year columns (FY runs Oct 1 - Sep 30, named by ending year).'

  dimension fiscal_year {
    label: 'Fiscal Year'
    type: 'number'
    description: 'Fiscal year number, named by the ending calendar year. Oct 2025 - Sep 2026 = 2026.'
    definition: @sql
      CASE WHEN CAST({{ #SOURCE.month_number }} AS INT) >= 10
           THEN CAST({{ #SOURCE.year }} AS INT) + 1
           ELSE CAST({{ #SOURCE.year }} AS INT)
      END ;;
  }

  dimension fiscal_year_label {
    label: 'Fiscal Year Label'
    type: 'text'
    description: "Display form of the fiscal year, e.g. 'FY26'. Alphabetical order is chronological order."
    definition: @sql 'FY' || CAST({{ fiscal_year }} - 2000 AS VARCHAR) ;;
  }

  dimension fiscal_quarter {
    label: 'Fiscal Quarter'
    type: 'text'
    description: "Fiscal quarter, with the calendar months spelled out so nobody has to remember that Q1 is Oct-Dec."
    definition: @sql
      CASE WHEN CAST({{ #SOURCE.month_number }} AS INT) >= 10 THEN 'Q1 (Oct-Dec)'
           WHEN CAST({{ #SOURCE.month_number }} AS INT) >= 7  THEN 'Q4 (Jul-Sep)'
           WHEN CAST({{ #SOURCE.month_number }} AS INT) >= 4  THEN 'Q3 (Apr-Jun)'
           ELSE 'Q2 (Jan-Mar)'
      END ;;
  }

  // Position of the month within the fiscal year: Oct = 1, Sep = 12.
  // Only needed to line two fiscal years up on a shared axis. Pair it
  // with the base model's `month_name_abbr` for the label, so the axis
  // reads 'Oct, Nov, Dec, ...' in real calendar months while sorting in
  // fiscal order. No shifted dates anywhere, so nothing to mentally
  // translate.
  dimension fiscal_period_number {
    label: 'Fiscal Period Number'
    type: 'number'
    description: 'Position of the calendar month within the fiscal year. Oct = 1, Sep = 12. Use as the sort key when comparing fiscal years side by side.'
    definition: @sql
      CASE WHEN CAST({{ #SOURCE.month_number }} AS INT) >= 10
           THEN CAST({{ #SOURCE.month_number }} AS INT) - 9
           ELSE CAST({{ #SOURCE.month_number }} AS INT) + 3
      END ;;
  }

  // The label form of the above. Zero-padded period number in front of
  // the real calendar month abbreviation: '01 Oct', '02 Nov', ... '12 Sep'.
  //
  // This exists because Holistics chart sorting addresses fields by
  // their POSITION IN THE VIZ (`sort { field_index: N }`) — there is no
  // "sort this text axis by that other numeric field". So an axis
  // labelled with bare month names sorts alphabetically: Apr, Aug, Dec.
  // Putting the sort key inside the label string is the only way to get
  // a chart axis that reads in real calendar months AND runs in fiscal
  // order, without also having to show a numeric column next to it.
  //
  // Costs one dimension in one file, and every fact entity and every
  // date role inherits it — including `seed_c_dates_signup`, which
  // re-declares nothing.
  dimension fiscal_period_label {
    label: 'Fiscal Period'
    type: 'text'
    description: "Fiscal period as '01 Oct' ... '12 Sep'. Real calendar months, but alphabetical order is fiscal order, so it sorts correctly on a chart axis."
    definition: @sql
      LPAD(CAST({{ fiscal_period_number }} AS VARCHAR), 2, '0')
        || ' ' || {{ #SOURCE.month_name_abbr }} ;;
  }

  dimension fiscal_year_offset {
    label: 'Fiscal Year Offset'
    type: 'number'
    description: '0 = the fiscal year containing today, -1 = the one before it. Resolves against CURRENT_DATE and is not affected by dashboard filters.'
    definition: @sql
      {{ fiscal_year }} - (
        CASE WHEN CAST(EXTRACT(MONTH FROM CURRENT_DATE) AS INT) >= 10
             THEN CAST(EXTRACT(YEAR FROM CURRENT_DATE) AS INT) + 1
             ELSE CAST(EXTRACT(YEAR FROM CURRENT_DATE) AS INT)
        END
      ) ;;
  }
})
